  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:\n`BuggyToast` completely fails its primary purpose for assistive technology by rendering dynamic status updates into a generic `<div>` with no accessible role, live region attributes, or programmatic announcement mechanism. Furthermore, its rigid 3-second auto-dismissal violates fundamental timing requirements by offering no mechanism to pause, extend, or manually dismiss the notification, stranding low-vision, screen reader, and cognitive users.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n*Predicted issues for dynamic toast / alert notifications prior to code inspection:*\n1. Missing `role=\"alert\"` / `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live`, resulting in complete silence for screen reader users upon notification appearance.\n2. Inadequate, unpausable auto-dismissal timer that disappears before users with cognitive disabilities or low vision can read it.\n3. Lack of manual keyboard dismissal mechanism (no close `<button>`, no `Escape` listener).\n4. Live-region lifecycle flaw: dynamically mounting an unannounced element rather than using a persistent live region container.\n*Outcome*: All predictions confirmed directly in the source code.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n1. **Dynamic status message has no accessible role or live region announcements (silent failure)**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyToast.jsx:19-21`\n     ```jsx\n     <div className=\"toast-notification\">\n       {message}\n     </div>\n     ```\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver, TalkBack).\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA), SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A); WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Alert Pattern & Status Pattern.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH.\n   - **Why this matters**: When the component renders, the DOM node appears silently. Assistive technology receives zero notification or dispatch event because the element lacks `role=\"alert\"`, `role=\"status\"`, or `aria-live`. Screen reader users performing an action (submitting a form, saving settings, triggering an error) are left unaware that an event occurred or failed.\n   - **Fix**: Add appropriate ARIA semantics based on message severity. For urgent/error messages, use `role=\"alert\"` (or `aria-live=\"assertive\"` and `aria-atomic=\"true\"`). For standard informational messages, use `role=\"status\"` (or `aria-live=\"polite\"`). Ensure the live container is persistent in the DOM before message injection so assistive technologies reliably detect text node mutations.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: toast-missing-live-region-role\nfingerprint: a1b2c3d4e5f67890\nsource: BuggyToast.jsx:19-21\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.3 Status Messages / WAI-ARIA APG Alert Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 Level AA equivalent (4.1.2 / 402.1)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, cognitive=LOW, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW\nevidence: <div className=\"toast-notification\">{message}</div> contains no role or aria-live attributes.\nreproduction_steps: Mount <BuggyToast message=\"Changes saved successfully\" /> in a running DOM tree with an active screen reader; observe spoken phrases log.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader immediately announces \"Changes saved successfully\" upon appearance (assertive or polite queue).\nactual_behavior: Complete silence; no speech output or braille terminal update occurs.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n1. **Unadjustable 3-second auto-dismissal violates timing guidelines and cannot be paused**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyToast.jsx:6-12`\n     ```jsx\n     useEffect(() => {\n       const timer = setTimeout(() => {\n         setIsVisible(false);\n       }, duration);\n\n       return () => clearTimeout(timer);\n     }, [duration]);\n     ```\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Cognitive accessibility users, low-vision users (screen magnifier users), screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable (Level A), SC 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide (Level A).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH.\n   - **Why this matters**: A fixed 3000ms duration provides insufficient time for users with reading difficulties, motor impairments, or cognitive delays to discover, read, and comprehend the message. Furthermore, low-vision users utilizing 200%-400% zoom or screen magnifiers may need time to pan across the screen to find where the toast rendered. Without mouse hover (`onMouseEnter`/`onMouseLeave`) or focus listeners (`onFocus`/`onBlur`) to pause the countdown, the toast vanishes before it can be read.\n   - **Fix**:\n     1. Add event listeners to pause the dismissal timeout on hover (`onPointerEnter`/`onPointerLeave`) and focus (`onFocus`/`onBlur`).\n     2. Allow users to adjust or disable the timeout, or persist critical notifications until explicitly dismissed.\n     3. Ensure a minimum default reading duration calculation (e.g., base time + words-per-minute formula) or at least 5000\u20138000ms for short messages, with indefinite duration for errors.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: toast-unadjustable-auto-dismiss\nfingerprint: f7e8d9c0b1a23456\nsource: BuggyToast.jsx:6-12\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable / SC 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 SC 2.2.1 / 2.2.2 (Level A)\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: cognitive=HIGH, low_vision=HIGH, screen_reader=MEDIUM, keyboard=MEDIUM\nevidence: setTimeout(..., 3000) automatically removes component after 3s with no pause on hover/focus and no option to extend.\nreproduction_steps: Trigger toast notification; view under screen magnifier or simulate slow reading speed.\nexpected_behavior: Notification stays visible while mouse hovers over it or user interacts, and gives user control to extend or retain time limit.\nactual_behavior: Toast vanishes after 3 seconds regardless of user reading progress, focus, or pointer location.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n2. **No interactive dismiss control or keyboard dismiss support**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyToast.jsx:18-22`\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users, cognitive users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A); WAI-ARIA APG Alert Pattern.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH.\n   - **Why this matters**: There is no dismiss button or keyboard handler (`Escape`). If a toast obscures underlying interface elements, sighted keyboard-only users cannot dismiss it on demand to view content beneath it.\n   - **Fix**: Provide an accessible dismiss button inside the toast with a semantic `<button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Dismiss notification\">` and handle the `Escape` key when focused.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n1. **Missing semantic notification type and visual iconography indicators**\n   - **Location**: `BuggyToast.jsx:19-21`\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Cognitive users, low-vision users, screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A), SC 1.4.1 Use of Color (Level A).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH.\n   - **Why this matters**: The component does not differentiate between notification types (e.g., success, warning, error, info). Sighted and AT users cannot discern the severity or category of the message from the markup.\n   - **Fix**: Accept a `type` prop (e.g. `'success' | 'error' | 'warning' | 'info'`), render an appropriate semantic icon or hidden prefix text (`<span className=\"sr-only\">Error: </span>`), and apply corresponding visual indicator classes that do not rely on color alone.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n1. **Persistent container pattern for React live regions**:\n   - In modern screen readers (notably NVDA and VoiceOver on iOS/macOS), dynamically mounting a node that *already* contains both `role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live` and the text message during initial mount can result in the announcement being dropped.\n   - *Best Practice*: Render a persistent live region container in the layout (e.g., `<div aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic=\"true\" className=\"toast-region\">`) that remains mounted, and dynamically append/update the toast message nodes inside it.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions)\n\n- **Missing live region wrapper / role**: No `role=\"alert\"`, `role=\"status\"`, or `aria-live`.\n- **Missing pause-on-hover and pause-on-focus**: No mechanism to halt the 3-second unmount timer when hovered or focused.\n- **Missing manual close control**: No native `<button>` element to dismiss the toast.\n- **Missing `Escape` key handling**: Toast cannot be dismissed via keyboard shortcut.\n- **Missing `aria-atomic=\"true\"`**: If updated dynamically, assistive technology may announce only fragments rather than the entire message.\n- **Missing variant context**: No indication whether the notification represents an error, warning, or success state.\n- **Screen reader virtual cursor trap on unmount**: When `isVisible` becomes false, unmounting `BuggyToast` abruptly removes the element from the DOM. If a screen reader user is actively reading the virtual buffer at that spot, the reading cursor is unexpectedly reset to the top of the document.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|:---|:---:|:---|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Missing `role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live`; status change is completely silent. |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **MEDIUM** | No dismiss button or keyboard interaction model (`Escape`). |\n| **Low vision** | **HIGH** | 3000ms duration is too brief to locate and read under screen magnification; no pause on hover. |\n| **Cognitive** | **HIGH** | Unadjustable time limit violates WCAG 2.2.1; automatic disappearance creates confusion. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No animation or motion triggers present in the code snippet. |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No auditory content or auto-playing media. |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **MEDIUM** | Depends on external `.toast-notification` CSS styles not defined in fixture. |\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Complete failure. An alert or toast exists solely to notify the user of an asynchronous event or status change. Without ARIA live region semantics, a user using NVDA, JAWS, or VoiceOver receives zero feedback.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: The user cannot interact with or dismiss the toast. While toasts typically do not steal focus on appearance, they must be dismissible if they overlay interactive page content.\n- **Low vision user**: Under 200%\u2013400% zoom or when using screen magnification software, the user's viewport covers only a fraction of the screen. A toast appearing in a corner and vanishing after 3 seconds will disappear before the user can pan to read it.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: People with reading disabilities, ADHD, or cognitive impairments require sufficient processing time. Vanishing messages without pause/dismiss controls increase cognitive friction and anxiety.\n\n---\n\n### Realist Check & Severity Calibration\n\n1. *Realistic worst case*: A user with a screen reader submits a critical payment or setting change, the server returns an error (\"Credit card expired\"), `BuggyToast` mounts silently, and disappears 3 seconds later. The user assumes the form submitted successfully and never learns about the failure.\n2. *Severity justification*:\n   - Missing live region semantics is calibrated at **CRITICAL** because it causes an absolute barrier to communication for screen reader users.\n   - Unadjustable 3-second auto-dismissal is calibrated at **MAJOR** because it severely degrades usability across low-vision and cognitive cohorts under WCAG 2.2.1 and 2.2.2.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REJECT**\n\nThe component cannot be accepted or revised with minor edits; it fails the core accessibility requirements of a notification component. To achieve an **ACCEPT** verdict, the component requires:\n1. ARIA live region semantics (`role=\"status\"` or `role=\"alert\"`, `aria-live`, `aria-atomic=\"true\"`), preferably utilizing a persistent container pattern.\n2. Timer controls: pause on hover (`pointerenter`/`pointerleave`), pause on focus (`focusin`/`focusout`), and an extended minimum reading duration.\n3. An accessible dismiss `<button>` with an appropriate accessible label and `Escape` key listener.\n4. Programmatic and visual identification of notification type (error, success, info).\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (unscored)\n\n- *Notification Type & Urgency*: Will `BuggyToast` be used for critical errors (requiring `role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live=\"assertive\"`) or routine notifications (requiring `role=\"status\"` / `aria-live=\"polite\"`)?\n- *Multiple Toasts / Toast Stacking*: If multiple toasts fire in rapid succession, is there a queue or toast manager to prevent notifications from overwriting each other in the live region?\n",
